\addvspace {10\p@ }
\contentsline {table}{\numberline {2.1}{\ignorespaces Source files of the implementation, classified by how the reference is used. The lattice core is reused unchanged; the only modified file is the build script; the scheme, serialization, application, and evaluation are new.}}{17}{table.2.1}%
\contentsline {table}{\numberline {2.2}{\ignorespaces Parameter sets used in the evaluation. The \emph {LAS-2020/845 reference} set is the original paper's parameter set; the \emph {Simplified Dilithium-II/III/V} sets reuse the dimensions of Dilithium modes~2/3/5 (so the public-key, secret, and challenge dimensions line up with NIST levels~2/3/5) and are engineering benchmark settings, \emph {not} formal NIST/ML-DSA security-equivalence claims. All lattice sets share the ring degree $N=256$ and modulus $q=8\,380\,417\approx 2^{23}$ inherited from the reused Dilithium NTT; \emph {Simplified Dilithium-III} is the project's target set. The classical secp256k1 ECDSA adaptor is a functionality-matched baseline at $\approx $128-bit classical security. \Cref {tab:notation} defines each symbol.}}{21}{table.2.2}%
\contentsline {table}{\numberline {2.3}{\ignorespaces Security and scheme parameters: the symbol, its meaning, and the value(s) it takes across the parameter sets, \emph {listed in the row order of} \cref {tab:params} (LAS-2020/845 reference; Simplified Dilithium-II; -III; -V). The paper's ring-degree symbol $d$ is written $N$ here, because the reused Dilithium NTT fixes $N=256$. The figure and table captions in \cref {ch:results} refer back to these names and values.}}{22}{table.2.3}%
\addvspace {10\p@ }
\contentsline {table}{\numberline {3.1}{\ignorespaces The adaptor-signature correctness contract, each clause checked and passed by \texttt {test\_contract} (corroborated at scale by the 1000-iteration functional suite on modes 2/3/5, the 256-instance serialization suite, and the pinned known-answer test).}}{24}{table.3.1}%
\contentsline {table}{\numberline {3.2}{\ignorespaces Key overhead summary at the \emph {Simplified Dilithium-III} (target) setting ($n{=}6$, $\ell {=}5$, $M{=}11$, $\kappa {=}49$, $\gamma {=}137\,984$, $N{=}256$, $q{=}8\,380\,417$); $\mu $s/op, mean $\pm $ sample SD over 10 runs $\times $ 1000 iterations (\texttt {bench\_levels}). Overhead is each adaptor operation over its basic analogue; KeyGen (and statement generation) is shared by the basic signature and LAS.}}{24}{table.3.2}%
\contentsline {table}{\numberline {3.3}{\ignorespaces Adaptor overhead is small at every parameter set (\texttt {bench\_levels}; mean over 10 runs $\times $ 1000 iters, Extract in $\mu $s). Percentages are each adaptor operation over its basic analogue.}}{25}{table.3.3}%
\contentsline {table}{\numberline {3.4}{\ignorespaces LAS objects by component (packed bytes; \texttt {bench\_levels}). The LAS-2020/845 reference set shares the Simplified Dilithium-II dimensions. The challenge $c$ is fixed; the response $z$ grows with $n+\ell $ and dominates the signature at every setting.}}{26}{table.3.4}%
\contentsline {table}{\numberline {3.5}{\ignorespaces Complete LAS vs.\ basic-signature comparison at the \emph {Simplified Dilithium-III} (target) setting ($n{=}6$, $\ell {=}5$, $M{=}11$, $\kappa {=}49$, $\gamma {=}137\,984$, $N{=}256$, $q{=}8\,380\,417$). \emph {Top:} communication --- packed bytes of every object. \emph {Bottom:} computation --- $\mu $s/op, mean $\pm $ sample SD over 10 runs $\times $ 1000 iterations (\texttt {bench\_levels}). ``Basic'' is the simplified Dilithium-style base signature; shared rows are byte- or cycle-identical in both. The adapted signature is itself a valid basic signature.}}{28}{table.3.5}%
\contentsline {table}{\numberline {3.6}{\ignorespaces Classical vs.\ post-quantum adaptor signature: same functionality, different security foundation. Classical secp256k1 ($\approx $128-bit classical) and LAS at the \emph {Simplified Dilithium-II} parameters. Timings $\mu $s/op (mean over 10 runs $\times $ 1000 iterations, same machine); sizes in bytes. KeyGen also produces the statement/witness, which take the public-key/secret-key size.}}{29}{table.3.6}%
